To create a recursive agent function, you would need to define another function that executes different actions based on whether a specific port is open or closed. This function would then be called within the KQLML code snippet. Here's an example of what such a recursive agent function might look like:
```
function recursiveAgent(agentName, action) {
    if (action === "analyze" && target.includes(agentName)) {
        alert(`Anomaly detected: ${agentName} is open.`);
    }
}

// Usage:
recursiveAgent("target", "executeCode");

// Execute the code using JavaScript
var result = executeKqml(agentCode);
result.then(() => console.log("Anomalies detected, please investigate!"));
```

This code snippet defines a recursive agent function `recursiveAgent` that checks if the target IP address contains an element with the specified name and if so, alerts an user about any anomalies detected. It then calls this function within another JavaScript function using the KQLML code snippet.
You would need to replace `"target"` with the actual IP address of the system being scanned in your KQLML code snippet.

